WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.059 --> 00:00:04.858
- We live in a beautiful
world surrounded by genius.

00:00:04.858 --> 00:00:08.240
(water gurgles)

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:11.901
Shark skin is antimicrobial
and reduces drag.

00:00:11.901 --> 00:00:16.410
Lotus leaf plants have
self-cleaning surfaces.

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:19.130
Peacock feathers create
color without pigment.

00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:23.060
Gecko foot pads create
adhesion without adhesives.

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:26.050
I've always been fascinated
by the natural world.

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:27.860
Number one, I see the inherent beauty,

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:30.739
but secondly, on a deeper
level I look and I see possible

00:00:30.739 --> 00:00:33.322
solution sets to design challenges.

00:00:37.572 --> 00:00:40.007
I'm Maria O'Farrell and I'm
a biomimicry professional.

00:00:40.007 --> 00:00:41.332
And what that means is it's my job

00:00:41.332 --> 00:00:43.274
to go into companies and help them solve

00:00:43.274 --> 00:00:45.851
design challenges using 3.8 billion years

00:00:45.851 --> 00:00:48.452
of research and development
from the natural world.

00:00:48.452 --> 00:00:51.369
(techno music)

00:00:51.369 --> 00:00:54.061
- My name's Kevin Beck
and I work at Lenovo.

00:00:54.061 --> 00:00:56.852
Engineering is ultimately
all about iteration.

00:00:56.852 --> 00:00:59.741
You rarely get anything
right the first time.

00:00:59.741 --> 00:01:01.252
You build it and you test it

00:01:01.252 --> 00:01:02.564
and you figure out what's wrong with it

00:01:02.564 --> 00:01:04.683
and then you improve it
and you do that again,

00:01:04.683 --> 00:01:05.986
and again, and again, and again,

00:01:05.986 --> 00:01:08.015
but nature's been doing
that for billions of years.

00:01:08.015 --> 00:01:09.986
So sometimes it helps
to sort of skip ahead

00:01:09.986 --> 00:01:14.066
in the process and see what
solution nature came up with.

00:01:14.066 --> 00:01:16.143
That's what biomimicry is all about;

00:01:16.143 --> 00:01:18.134
taking the best of nature and seeing

00:01:18.134 --> 00:01:22.301
if we can somehow adapt that
to solve a technology problem.

00:01:23.186 --> 00:01:25.363
So here's a good example
of how this works.

00:01:25.363 --> 00:01:27.186
We've got a thermal and cooling engineer,

00:01:27.186 --> 00:01:29.288
who's faced with a
reasonably large challenge

00:01:29.288 --> 00:01:31.094
of creating a new type of fan

00:01:31.094 --> 00:01:33.756
to go into a new all-in-one desktop.

00:01:33.756 --> 00:01:36.375
By necessity it has to
be really, really thin,

00:01:36.375 --> 00:01:38.636
but also be really, really quiet.

00:01:38.636 --> 00:01:39.615
So this engineer took his son

00:01:39.615 --> 00:01:42.334
to the aquarium on a Saturday.

00:01:42.334 --> 00:01:44.751
And there was a dolphin show.

00:01:45.924 --> 00:01:47.625
(dolphin clicks)

00:01:47.625 --> 00:01:49.249
So he's sitting there
watching the dolphin show

00:01:49.249 --> 00:01:51.220
and he notices that when
they come out of the water

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:52.101
and they go back into the water,

00:01:52.101 --> 00:01:54.243
it's a very clean exit
and a very clean entry

00:01:54.243 --> 00:01:56.391
because of the shape of their body.

00:01:56.391 --> 00:01:58.203
- Dolphins evolved a design strategy

00:01:58.203 --> 00:02:00.234
so that they could move
through the water quickly

00:02:00.234 --> 00:02:03.312
with the least amount of
noise to capture their prey.

00:02:03.312 --> 00:02:05.654
- He had this kind of
ah-ha moment that said,

00:02:05.654 --> 00:02:08.351
hmm, if they're that efficient in water,

00:02:08.351 --> 00:02:12.032
I wonder if that structure might
also work for a fan in air?

00:02:12.032 --> 00:02:14.670
- Water has 500 times the
frictional drag that air does.

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:16.629
So if you found something
that can do it in water,

00:02:16.629 --> 00:02:17.679
it stands to reason that you found

00:02:17.679 --> 00:02:20.959
a pretty good design for the air.

00:02:20.959 --> 00:02:22.571
- So he goes back and sort of mocks up

00:02:22.571 --> 00:02:24.709
this design in his CAD program.

00:02:24.709 --> 00:02:26.707
(whimsical music)

00:02:26.707 --> 00:02:27.879
It didn't work very well at all,

00:02:27.879 --> 00:02:29.628
the first, second, third,
fourth, fifth time,

00:02:29.628 --> 00:02:30.897
but he finally got to something

00:02:30.897 --> 00:02:34.230
that actually works really, really well.

00:02:35.545 --> 00:02:36.887
There's a fan blade and on the ends

00:02:36.887 --> 00:02:39.430
of the tips of the blades,
there’s a little structure

00:02:39.430 --> 00:02:42.036
that looks kinda like a dolphin's body

00:02:42.036 --> 00:02:46.464
that are very efficient at
moving air and very, very quiet.

00:02:46.464 --> 00:02:47.454
There are a number of other examples

00:02:47.454 --> 00:02:50.156
of biomimicry in Lenovo products as well.

00:02:50.156 --> 00:02:53.956
Stands inspired by the
shape of an orchid stem.

00:02:53.956 --> 00:02:56.700
Fans inspired by the
shape of an owl's wing.

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:57.887
(air rushes against wings)

00:02:57.887 --> 00:02:59.647
Cooling vents inspired by

00:02:59.647 --> 00:03:02.527
the honeycomb structure of a beehive.

00:03:02.527 --> 00:03:05.625
Each of these designs have all in some way

00:03:05.625 --> 00:03:09.004
been made better by biomimicry.

00:03:09.004 --> 00:03:11.646
- We are natural beings, we are animals.

00:03:11.646 --> 00:03:12.554
And I think a lot of the problems

00:03:12.554 --> 00:03:14.716
that we face are tied
indelibly to the fact

00:03:14.716 --> 00:03:18.185
that we have divorced
ourselves from nature.

00:03:18.185 --> 00:03:20.265
But the good news is,
again as a young species,

00:03:20.265 --> 00:03:22.519
that we have a lot of mentors
in evolutionary elders

00:03:22.519 --> 00:03:26.039
and a life's operating
manual that we can consult.

00:03:26.039 --> 00:03:28.438
All of the solutions we seek
out are right in front of us.

00:03:28.438 --> 00:03:29.878
We're surrounded by genius.

00:03:29.878 --> 00:03:32.461
(techno music)

